  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A wavering frontal boundary will bring occasional \nshowers and a few thunderstorms through Thursday night. \n\nThis morning a convective shortwave trough is bringing nocturnal \nshowers and thunderstorms, with activity focused upon a cold frontal \nboundary to our west. Convection early this morning across Lake Erie \nand the Southern Tier may expand northward and clip the Buffalo \nmetro region, before activity wanes with warming cloud tops across \nthe Genesee Valley just past sunrise.  \n\nA cold front will slip southward across our region this early \nafternoon, and while convection ahead of the front will be isolated \nand focused upon our southern zones, a noted wind shift will occur \nbehind the front with a northerly wind component. This front will \nstall near the state line tonight, with an isolated shower or \nthunderstorm possible across the Southern Tier. \n\nLater in the night a weak surface low upon this frontal boundary \nwill shift the front back northward as a warm front early Thursday \nmorning. In a moisture rich environment with a deep warm cloud depth \nheavy rain is a possibility. Pwats become greater than 1.75 inches \nto around 2.00 inches and near the 90th percentile or even possible \nrecord values per Buffalo sounding climatology for this time of \nyear, and a parallel mid level flow to the frontal boundary may \nencourage training storms, with a potential for localized ponding \nof water in heavier activity. There is still uncertainty with the \naxis of where the heavy rain could occur, whether near the state \nline, or farther northward. A surface low will pass through WNY \nThursday with low level convergence generating additional showers \nand thunderstorms until a cold front slices across our region \nThursday afternoon, bringing an end to convection. \n\nThe timing of convection, generally in the morning or overnight, \ncombined with weak to modest instability will lend to a low severe \nthreat, though the moisture rich environment could pose a hydrologic \nissue with heavier storms, potentially passing over the same areas \nThursday morning.  \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A brief period of drier, cooler conditions is \nexpected Thursday night through Friday night. \n\nSurface high pressure will bring fair, dry weather Thursday night \nthrough Friday night with dendritic river valley fog likely across \nthe Southern Tier and Black River valleys. It may even become cold \nenough Friday night, that if winds become calm patches of frost will \nbe possible in the traditionally colder areas of the inland North \nCountry. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Unsettled pattern this weekend and into the \nbeginning of next week. \n\nAn unsettled pattern emerges Saturday as a warm front lifts back \nnorthward across our region as surface high pressure exits to the \neast. Several additional shortwave troughs will ripple across our \nregion through the weekend and into the beginning of next week \nmaintaining chances for showers and perhaps a few thunderstorms. \nEnsembles data from the GEFS, GEPS and ENS depict Saturday night \ninto Sunday morning with the greatest chances for precipitation, \nthis as a weak surface low expands northeastward from the Ohio \nValley along with developing weak instability. Several additional \nshortwave troughs and the eventual passage of an open wave trough \n(currently featured as a closed low over the PACNW) will maintain \nchances for showers through the first half of the new work week.",
